---
import BaseLayout from "~/layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import Container  from "@nl06/design-system/components/Container.astro";
import katex from "katex";
import "katex/dist/katex.min.css";

// Server-side KaTeX rendering — zero client JS.
function tex(src: string, displayMode = true): string {
  return katex.renderToString(src, {
    displayMode,
    throwOnError: false,
    output: "htmlAndMathml",
    strict: "ignore",
  });
}

const eq_a = tex(`a = \\left(\\mu / n^2\\right)^{1/3}`);
const eq_dv_tangential = tex(`\\Delta v \\approx \\tfrac{1}{2}\\, n\\, \\Delta a`);
const eq_dv_plane = tex(`\\Delta v \\approx 2\\,v\\,\\sin\\!\\left(\\Delta i / 2\\right)`);
const inl_mu = tex(`\\mu = 398600.4418\\ \\text{km}^3/\\text{s}^2`, false);
const inl_n = tex(`n`, false);
const inl_dv = tex(`\\Delta v`, false);
const inl_di = tex(`\\Delta i`, false);
const inl_v = tex(`v`, false);
---
<BaseLayout
  title="How this works — SSA"
  description="The math behind the maneuver detector: SGP4 element history, MAD-based jump detection, and Δv estimation."
  pathname="/method"
  itemNumber="№ 002"
>
  <article class="method">
    <Container size="prose" class="method__inner">

      <header class="method__head">
        <p class="method__back">
          <a href="/">← back to the feed</a>
        </p>
        <h1 class="method__title">How this works</h1>
      </header>

      <section class="prose">
        <p>
          Every active satellite is tracked from the ground, and the resulting
          orbits are published as element sets. Without a maneuver, an orbit
          changes only in predictable ways: atmospheric drag slowly lowers it,
          the Earth&rsquo;s equatorial bulge rotates its plane, and the Sun and
          Moon perturb it. These changes are smooth. A maneuver &mdash; a
          thruster firing &mdash; produces a sudden break in an otherwise
          smooth curve. This page reads the published orbits of active payloads
          and flags those breaks.
        </p>

        <h2>The measurement</h2>
        <p>
          For each satellite we turn its published mean motion into a
          semi-major axis &mdash; the size of the orbit &mdash; and read its
          inclination &mdash; the tilt of its plane:
        </p>
        <div class="eq" set:html={eq_a} />
        <p>
          where <span set:html={inl_mu} /> and <span set:html={inl_n} /> is the
          mean motion in radians per second. We track both over a rolling
          90-day window. A burn along the direction of travel changes the
          orbit&rsquo;s size and energy; a burn out of plane changes its tilt.
        </p>

        <h2>Finding the break</h2>
        <p>
          We remove the smooth decay each orbit is already following; what
          remains is noise, plus the occasional step. We measure each
          satellite&rsquo;s own noise level &mdash; robustly, so a real
          maneuver can&rsquo;t inflate it &mdash; and flag a step only when it
          clears that noise by a set margin (three standard deviations by
          default). Independently, we propagate each published orbit forward
          to the moment of the next one and measure how far the prediction
          misses: between two honest, maneuver-free orbits the miss is small;
          a burn in between throws it off. When both tests agree, the
          detection is marked high-confidence.
        </p>

        <h2>Estimating the push</h2>
        <p>
          From the change in the orbit we estimate the velocity change &mdash;
          the <span set:html={inl_dv} /> &mdash; behind each maneuver. For a
          burn along track,
        </p>
        <div class="eq" set:html={eq_dv_tangential} />
        <p>
          and for a change of orbital plane by angle <span set:html={inl_di} />
          at orbital speed <span set:html={inl_v} />,
        </p>
        <div class="eq" set:html={eq_dv_plane} />
        <p>
          A geostationary satellite nudging back into its slot spends fractions
          of a metre per second; one changing orbits entirely spends tens or
          hundreds. The number you see is an estimate, not telemetry.
        </p>

        <h2>What this is not</h2>
        <p>
          This is inference from public tracking data, and it has honest
          limits. The published orbit takes time to settle after a burn, so
          the detected moment lags the real one, sometimes by days. A
          geomagnetic storm thickens the upper atmosphere and drags many
          satellites down at once, which can mimic a small maneuver &mdash;
          because it hits a whole population rather than one object, we treat
          shared dips as weather and keep only object-specific breaks. And the
          catalog itself carries noise. So read each entry as &ldquo;this
          orbit changed in a way that looks deliberate,&rdquo; not as a claim
          about intent, payload, or operator. It is one way to track
          maneuvers from public data.
        </p>
      </section>

      <footer class="method__foot" aria-hidden="true">
        <span class="asterism">⁂</span>
      </footer>

    </Container>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .method { padding-top: var(--space-12); padding-bottom: var(--space-20); }
  .method__inner {
    padding-left: var(--space-page);
    padding-right: var(--space-page);
  }

  .method__back {
    margin: 0 0 var(--space-4) 0;
    font-family: var(--font-sans);
    font-size: var(--type-marginalia);
    letter-spacing: var(--tracking-meta);
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--color-ink-muted);
    font-weight: 500;
  }
  .method__back a {
    color: inherit;
    text-decoration: none;
    border-bottom: 1px solid color-mix(in srgb, currentColor 40%, transparent);
  }
  .method__back a:hover { color: var(--color-accent); border-bottom-color: var(--color-accent); }

  .method__title {
    margin: 0 0 var(--space-8) 0;
    font-family: var(--font-serif);
    font-style: italic;
    font-weight: 700;
    font-size: clamp(2.5rem, 6vw, 4rem);
    color: var(--color-accent);
    letter-spacing: var(--tracking-display);
    line-height: 1.05;
  }

  .prose {
    font-family: var(--font-serif);
    font-size: var(--type-body);
    line-height: var(--leading-prose);
    color: var(--color-ink-soft);
  }
  .prose h2 {
    margin: var(--space-10) 0 var(--space-3) 0;
    font-family: var(--font-serif);
    font-style: italic;
    font-weight: 600;
    font-size: var(--type-h3);
    color: var(--color-ink);
    letter-spacing: var(--tracking-tight);
  }
  .prose p {
    margin: 0 0 var(--space-5) 0;
  }
  .prose p:first-of-type::first-letter {
    font-family: var(--font-serif);
    font-style: italic;
    font-weight: 700;
    font-size: 3.6em;
    line-height: 0.85;
    float: left;
    color: var(--color-accent);
    margin: 0.05em 0.08em -0.1em 0;
    font-feature-settings: var(--features-display);
  }

  /* KaTeX block equations — paper-cream box, accent color for the math. */
  .eq {
    margin: var(--space-5) 0;
    padding: var(--space-4) var(--space-5);
    background: var(--color-paper-dim);
    border-left: 2px solid var(--color-accent);
    overflow-x: auto;
    font-size: 1.05em;
  }
  .eq :global(.katex) { color: var(--color-ink); }
  .eq :global(.katex-display) { margin: 0; }

  .method__foot {
    margin-top: var(--space-16);
    padding-top: var(--space-8);
    border-top: 1px solid var(--color-rule);
    text-align: center;
  }
  .asterism {
    font-size: 1.5rem;
    color: var(--color-ink-faint);
    letter-spacing: 0.5em;
  }
</style>
